--  Calling an internal command.
--
--  An internal command is called the way Ada calls a procedure: a name, then a
--  semicolon. Not a bare word. That is the difference a user notices first,
--  and it is deliberate -- there is one grammar here, and commands live inside
--  it rather than beside it.
--
--  A command is a call, so a submission mixes them with everything else: a
--  declaration before one is still in scope after it, a command may stand
--  inside a loop or an `if`, and `quit (Total);` ends a script with a value it
--  computed. `command-in-control-flow.adash` and `compute-and-exit.adash` are
--  those two, and this one is the smallest form there is.

version;

--  What a command answers with is its status, in the same way a program's is.
--  A command that failed does not stop what follows it -- Ada does not end a
--  sequence because a procedure reported a failure, and neither does `sh -c`.
--  A caller that wants a sequence to stop writes the test.
Before : Integer := Status;

version;

if Status = Before then
   put_line ("and both said the same about how they went");
end if;
